<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Winthrop</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1588">1588</a>–<a href="/years/1649">1649</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Winthrop was an English Puritan lawyer who led the Winthrop Fleet of about 1,000 settlers to Massachusetts in 1630 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>. Governor of the Massachusetts Bay Colony for most of its first two decades, he described the colony as a 'city upon a hill'. His journal is a main source for early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-winthrop.jpg" alt="John Winthrop" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author; when donated to the AAS, it was thought to be by a follower of Anthony van Dyck … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Winthrop_17th_cent._American_Antiquarian_Society_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>John Winthrop</b> led a fleet of about 1,000 settlers in 1630, founding Boston.</blockquote><a href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Edwardstone, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.04,0.83&amp;year=1627" aria-label="Edwardstone on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4569 365 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Winthrop"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5019 635h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5019 635h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.04,0.83&amp;year=1627">What was here in 1627? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For wee must consider that wee shall be as a citty upon a hill. The eies of all people are uppon us.&#8221;</p><footer>A Model of Christian Charity (1630) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Model_of_Christian_Charity" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Wee must delight in eache other; make other's conditions our oune; rejoice together, mourne together, labour and suffer together, allwayes haueving before our eyes our commission and community in the worke, as members of the same body.&#8221;</p><footer>A Model of Christian Charity (1630) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Model_of_Christian_Charity" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now the onely way to avoyde this shipwracke, and to provide for our posterity, is to followe the counsell of Micah, to doe justly, to love mercy, to walk humbly with our God.&#8221;</p><footer>A Model of Christian Charity (1630) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Model_of_Christian_Charity" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
